# Clean architecture

Cabbie is onion: inner layers do not know outer ones. Read the code that exists; do not clone Wolverine’s catalog.

## Layers

| Layer | Lives in | May know | Must not |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Domain | `src/Cabbie/Domain` | nothing outer | I/O, handlers, reflection catalogs, adapter packages |
| Application | `src/Cabbie/Application` | Domain | Npgsql, Rabbit, HTTP, EF; no `ValueObjects/` folders |
| Infrastructure | `src/Cabbie/Infrastructure` | Application ports | adapter package refs (those belong in adapter projects) |
| Adapters | `Cabbie.Postgresql`, `.RabbitMQ`, `.Http` | Cabbie core | leaking broker/DB types into core |

`src/Cabbie` must not ProjectReference adapter projects. FluentValidation is allowed in core; Npgsql stays in `Cabbie.Postgresql`.

## Taxonomy

- `ValueObjects/` and Domain-shaped named vocabulary live under Domain only (`Envelope`, `Messaging`, `Sagas`, `Errors`).
- Application owns ports, catalogs, and orchestration records at the feature-folder root (`Executor`, `DiscoveredHandler`, `IErrorQueue`).
- Namespaces follow folders.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — Application owning Domain vocabulary
namespace Cabbie.Application.Execution.ValueObjects;
public sealed record Retry : ErrorAction;

// ✅ GOOD — named recovery is Domain; Application applies it
namespace Cabbie.Domain.Errors.ValueObjects;
public sealed record Retry : ErrorAction;
```

## Messaging shape

Handlers never call each other. They return messages. The bus wraps an `Envelope` and either `Invoke`s now or `Publish`es. Dependencies on handler types are constructor-only; method parameters are the message, `CancellationToken`, and optionally `Envelope`.
